This PR adds vector operations and physical expressions.

# Vector Operations

## Overview
We have two types of physical operations (Op) unary and binary Ops and each are called as:
VectorOperations::UnaryOp(operand, result);
VectorOperations::BinaryOp(left, right, result);
Type specifies whether the operation is Boolean e.g., And, Comparison e.g., Equals, or Arithmetic e.g., Power. Operations are only defined between vectors. An operation between a vector and a literal is an operation between a vector and a flat vector of capacity 1 with its currPos set to 0.

## Functors
In our code, we define functors, which are function objects used for parameterization.
For instance we define the following functor:

```
 struct Add {
   template<class TA, class TB>
   static inline auto operation(TA left, TB right) {
     return left + right;
   }
 };
```

As such we can use the object Add as a function: Add(). Most of our functors of the same category such as  arithmetic ones contain the same signature and therefore cannot be contained in the same struct. We create a struct per operation. These structs are defined in the headers boolean_operations.h, arithmetic_operations.h, and comparison_operations.h.

The functors we define so far are mappers. That is each value or two value(s) provided as input get mapped to a new value. The operations are:
Arithmetic: Add, Subtract, Multiply, Divide, Modulo, Power.
Boolean: And, Or, Xor, Not
Comparison: Equals, NotEquals, LessThan, LessThanEquals, Greater

## Executors
Since we operate on vectors using the functors in almost the exact fashion, that is executing the same loop but with different functions called on each two elements. We define a UnaryOperationExecutor and a BinaryOperationExecutor which are for UnaryOp(s) and BinaryOp(s), respectively. These two executors are general and are classes with parameterized methods that execute a given functor with a specified input type(s) and output type. The binary executor considers when the left and right operands are both flat, both unflat, or one of them only is flat.

 ```
 struct BinaryOperationExecutor {
   // TA (left operand type), TB (right operand type), TR (result type)
   template<class TA, class TB, class TR, class FUNC = std::function<TR(TA, TB)>>
   static void execute(ValueVector& left, ValueVector& right, ValueVector& result) {
     ...
   }
 };
```
 
```
 struct UnaryOperationExecutor {
   // TO (operand type), TR (result type)
   template<class TO, class TR, class FUNC = std::function<TR(TO)>>
   static void execute(ValueVector& operand, ValueVector& result) {
       ...
   }
 };
```

### Vector Operations

VectorOperations::UnaryOp and VectorOperations::BinaryOp rely on the Unary/BinaryOperationExecutor to execute their methods and each VectorTypeOperations.cpp file creates another executor to specify the types on which to execute and when to throw exceptions. 

# Physical Expressions
Physical Expressions are general and are designed to be used beyond filters, but also for aggregations and the return clause. Physical Expressions are defined recursively in a tree. Expressions are either Binary or Unary expressions, they first call their children and then apply the operation on the result. Since we operate on lists, we note that each expression node is called once per call to the filter operator.
